---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../../components/Sequence.astro";
import { textChoices } from "../../lib/source.ts";

const M = "server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py";
const Q = "server/src/iki_server/runs/queue.py";
const A = "server/src/iki_server/api/worker.py";
const J = "worker/crates/iki-worker/src/job.rs";
const runStates = textChoices(M, "RunStatus");
const jobStates = textChoices(M, "JobState");

const H = {
  short: "What the worker is",
  how: "Registering and leasing",
  rules: "States, heartbeats, retries and cancelling",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "protocol", label: "One job between worker, server and storage" },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      <code>iki-worker</code> is one static Rust binary for every tier (<Adr n="0010" />). It
      registers once, then long-polls the server for a job it can run. Each lease carries a new
      token and lasts 60 seconds unless a heartbeat renews it; every report names the token, so a
      worker that was cut off can't change what replaced it. Nothing connects in to a worker. An
      attempt that ends badly is retried as a new <code>Job</code>, never by reusing the old one (<Adr
        n={[43, 46]}
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <h3>Registering</h3>
    <p>
      A worker registers with a Compute admin's registration token (<code>iki_wreg_…</code>), or, on
      a machine Iki started, with that launch's one-use token (<code>iki_wlch_…</code>). It gets its
      own token (<code>iki_wkr_…</code>) and states its capacity and the plugin code and environments
      it has. What a launched machine may run comes from its launch, not from what it says: its
      environment, image, plugin commit and template (<Path p={A} sym="def register(" />, <Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/runs/workers.py"
      />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/launches.py" />).
    </p>

    <h3>Leasing</h3>
    <p>
      <code>POST /worker/v1/lease</code> waits up to 30 seconds; each web process holds one{" "}
      <code>LISTEN</code> connection for every worker waiting on it, and a missed notification costs
      only the rest of the wait (<Path p={A} sym="MAX_WAIT = 30.0" />, <Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/runs/wakeup.py"
      />). The lease then picks a queued job on the worker's tier that:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>fits its capacity (a Slurm cluster's worker leases by its templates instead);</li>
      <li>
        was launched with plugin code the worker has, in an environment it has built (<PageLink
          to="Pinning and provenance"
        />);
      </li>
      <li>needs the reference data builds its machine has, and, on a later attempt, has its checkpoint checked;</li>
      <li>on a machine Iki started, is for its environment, image and kind of machine;</li>
      <li>on a self-hosted worker, is in a project its teams cover and needs no label it lacks (<Adr n="0112" />);</li>
      <li>isn't in a scope at its running quota or past an enforced budget (<PageLink to="Quotas and cost" />).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Of those, it weighs each project's next few jobs, projects with fewer jobs running first, then
      priority, then age, and locks one with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>, so workers never
      wait on each other (<Adr n="0108" />, <Path p={Q} sym="def _shortlist(" />). Before handing it
      over it checks the run again: the launcher must still have the tier's permission, the plugin
      must still be attached, and the run's project must still be able to read everything pinned for
      it (<PageLink to="Unreadable references" />). A run that fails one ends without starting. For a
      batchable action, up to <code>batchable - 1</code> more queued jobs of the same batch, code,
      image, tier and environment are packed into the leased one (<Adr n="0055" />, <Path
        p={Q}
        sym="def _pack("
      />). The job gets a fresh <code>lease_token</code> and its expiry (<Path p={Q} sym="def lease(" />,{" "}
      <Path p={Q} sym="LEASE_TTL = timedelta(seconds=60)" />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="protocol">One job between worker, server and storage</h3>
    <p>
      The calls between a worker, the server and object storage for one job. Paths are constants in
      the generated protocol crate; the worker API's full route list is on <PageLink
        to="Routes"
        hash="worker">Routes</PageLink
      >.
    </p>
    <Sequence
      id="wire"
      title="One job between worker, server and storage"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "cmd", label: "Command", sub: "the action's process" },
        { id: "worker", label: "iki-worker" },
        { id: "server", label: "Server", sub: "/worker/v1" },
        { id: "storage", label: "Object storage" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { phase: "Before any job" },
        { id: "register", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "POST register (or PUT me): capacity, plugin code" },
        { id: "plugins", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "GET plugins/{name}/{sha}, if it fetches code" },
        { phase: "One job" },
        { id: "lease", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "POST lease?wait=20" },
        { id: "input", from: "server", to: "worker", label: "200 run-input/v1 with lease_token, or 204", dashed: true },
        { id: "beat", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "heartbeat every 20 s; watch; logs" },
        { id: "files", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "POST jobs/{id}/files/{file}: a signed URL" },
        { id: "get", from: "worker", to: "storage", label: "GET the bytes; check size and SHA-256" },
        { id: "spawn", from: "worker", to: "cmd", label: "run, with IKI_INPUT, IKI_OUTPUT, IKI_SEED" },
        { id: "out", from: "cmd", to: "worker", label: "output.json, files in IKI_OUT_DIR", dashed: true },
        { id: "up", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "POST uploads: part URLs" },
        { id: "put", from: "worker", to: "storage", label: "PUT each part" },
        { id: "complete", from: "worker", to: "server", label: "POST complete {lease_token, output}" },
        { id: "status", from: "server", to: "worker", label: "run_status: succeeded, failed, rejected…", dashed: true },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="register">
        <p>The answer is the worker's own token. The plugin code it states decides which runs it may lease (<Adr n={[43, 47]} />).</p>
        <p><Path p={A} sym="def register(" />, <Path p={A} sym="def update_me(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="plugins">
        <p>Workers on people's machines and container pods fetch the plugin bundle by commit and check its SHA-256, then build its environment (<Adr n="0059" />).</p>
        <p><Path p={A} sym="def download_plugin(" />, <Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/bundles.rs" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="lease">
        <p>An async long poll: the server waits up to 30 s; this worker asks for 20.</p>
        <p><Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/worker.rs" sym="const LEASE_WAIT" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="input">
        <p>
          The payload names each input and included record at its pinned revision with its values,
          the plugin's name and commit, params, settings, files, proxy and secrets, and the job's
          timeout and resources. The <code>input.json</code> the command reads keeps only the job's
          ID, timeout and resources of that <code>job</code> section (<Adr n="0054" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={Q} sym="def payload(" />, <Path p={J} sym="fn stage_input(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="beat">
        <p>Each report carries the <code>lease_token</code>; a stale one gets 409 <code>stale_lease</code>. <code>watch</code> waits for a cancel, so it reaches the command at once.</p>
        <p><Path p={Q} sym="def heartbeat(" />, <Path p={Q} sym="def _holds(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="files">
        <p>Only files the job's run input lists can be fetched, by URLs good for five minutes (<Adr n="0066" />).</p>
        <p><Path p={A} sym="def download(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/input_files.py" sym="def download(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="get">
        <p>The worker fetches straight from storage with no Iki credentials, and checks what it got.</p>
        <p><Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/files.rs" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="spawn">
        <p>
          The command runs in its own process group from the plugin's directory, with only the
          variables it needs, never the worker's token. Its network goes through the egress proxy
          (<PageLink to="Network and secrets" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={J} sym={'"IKI_SEED"'} />, <Path p={J} sym="fn supervise(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="out">
        <p>A creates file (JSON Lines) can carry millions of records; the server stages it in chunks and writes it in one transaction (<Adr n="0056" />).</p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/imports.py" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="up">
        <p>Uploads land in object storage like any other upload (<PageLink to="Where data is stored" />).</p>
        <p><Path p={A} sym="def start_upload(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="put"><p>Multipart, straight to the bucket.</p></div>
      <div slot="complete">
        <p>A failing command posts <code>fail</code> with <code>failed</code>, <code>timed_out</code>, <code>interrupted</code> or <code>cancelled</code> instead.</p>
        <p><Path p={A} sym="def complete(" />, <Path p={Q} sym="def complete(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="status">
        <p><code>running</code> means a bulk import is still committing; <code>parked</code> means the run waits on an outside API's job (<Adr n="0130" />).</p>
      </div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Run and job states" open>
        <p>Read from <Path p={M} sym="class RunStatus" /> and <Path p={M} sym="class JobState" />:</p>
        <div class="pair">
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Note in the code</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{runStates.map((s) => <tr><td><code>{s.value}</code></td><td>{s.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Note in the code</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{jobStates.map((s) => <tr><td><code>{s.value}</code></td><td>{s.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Heartbeats move a job only forward">
        <p>
          A heartbeat renews the lease and may move the job on: leased, staging, pending (only on a
          Slurm cluster), running, uploading. It never goes back. A Slurm cluster's leases last ten
          minutes, so a login node can reboot without losing a job (<Path p={Q} sym="PHASES = (" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/slurm.py" sym="LEASE_TTL = timedelta(minutes=10)" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Retries are new jobs">
        <p>
          When an attempt ends with a reason in the run's <code>retry_on</code> and attempts remain,
          a new <code>Job</code> is queued with the next attempt number, 10 seconds times the attempt
          later. By default an action is retried twice on <code>worker_lost</code> or{" "}
          <code>interrupted</code>, and not at all if it declares hosts or outside APIs (<Path
            p="spec/src/iki_spec/actions.py"
            sym="retry_max="
          />, <Path p={Q} sym="RETRY_BACKOFF = timedelta(seconds=10)" />). Otherwise the reason
          decides how the run ends: a lost worker, an interruption, an unavailable machine or an
          unplaceable job ends it failed; losing permission ends it rejected; a detached plugin ends
          it cancelled (<Path p={Q} sym="def _end_attempt(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Lost workers and timeouts">
        <p>
          The task runner ends attempts whose lease ran out as interrupted, and those running five
          minutes past their timeout as timed out (<Path p={Q} sym="def recover_expired(" />, <Path
            p={Q}
            sym="TIMEOUT_GRACE = timedelta(minutes=5)"
          />). The worker itself stops a command on cancel, timeout or a lost lease: SIGTERM to its
          process group, then SIGKILL after 10 seconds (<Path p={J} sym="pub const STOP_GRACE" />).
          Revoking a worker interrupts its jobs, which are retried elsewhere.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Cancelling">
        <p>
          Whoever may launch the run's tier in its project may cancel it. A run no worker holds ends
          cancelled at once; otherwise its worker, watching the job, stops the command within a
          second. A packed job stops once every run in it is cancelled (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/runs/services.py"
            sym="def cancel("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <h3>The worker API</h3>
    <Routes doc="worker" />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={Q} /></li>
          <li><Path p={A} /></li>
          <li><Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src" /></li>
          <li><Path p="worker/crates/iki-protocol/src/generated.rs" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0010" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0043" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0046" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0108" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Where the ADRs and code differ</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0108" /> names an index <code>iki_job_queued_by_project</code>; the code's is <code>iki_job_queued_by_labels</code>, since <Adr n="0112" /> added labels.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
